  <h2>Agentic AI for IT operations</h2>
  <p>Agentic AI means an AI model that does more than answer questions: it chooses which tools to call, reads what they return and decides what to check next. The Model Context Protocol (MCP) is an open standard for giving a model a set of tools like this. A RAG pipeline (retrieval-augmented generation) lets a model look up your own documents before it answers.</p>
  <p>I build this kind of tooling for end-user computing operations: MCP servers, RAG pipelines and diagnostic agents for Azure Virtual Desktop and Citrix environments. An example is an agent that follows one user problem from the session, through the session host and FSLogix, down to the profile storage, to find the root cause.</p>
  <p>The rules I build by:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Read-only first.</strong> A diagnostic tool reads and reports. Fixing things comes later, in a controlled way, if at all.</li>
    <li><strong>Real APIs, checked against official documentation.</strong> Every call is verified before it is used, and no tool runs arbitrary commands.</li>
    <li><strong>One tool at a time.</strong> Each tool is built, tested and approved before the next one starts.</li>
    <li><strong>No production claims without evidence.</strong> A tool is not called production-ready until it has been tested in a real, authorized environment.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>These projects are in development and are not published as public repositories yet. I will link them here when they are.</p>
  <h2>About this site</h2>
  <p>AdnanSiddiqui.com is a technical publication. Articles are written in a fixed format: the problem, a simple explanation, a diagram, what has been seen in production, the repo files, and the sources.</p>
  <p>Azure facts are checked against Microsoft Learn before an article is published. Examples use made-up organizations, never real company names. This is a personal blog: opinions are my own and the site is not affiliated with Microsoft.</p>
